  "repeats": [],
  "notes": [
   "Breadboard hole names are approximated to the catalogue scheme: the image labels the two halves 'a-e' (bottom of image) and 'f-j' (top of image), which is flipped relative to the catalogue where top bank 't' = rows a-e. I mapped the image's lower half (a-e) to 't' and the upper half (f-j) to 'b' per the catalogue's convention.",
   "Pushbutton straddles the centre channel with legs in columns 5 and 7 (the wide pair). Side 1 (1.l/1.r) is in the lower half (a-e) and side 2 (2.l/2.r) is in the upper half (f-j), so the two sides are on different strips and pressing joins them.",
   "Blue wire runs from Arduino pin 2 to the upper half (row i/j area) at about column 6; that strip is the same column-bank as the button's side-2 legs only if it shares a column with them. In the image the blue wire ends at roughly column 6/7 in row i, so it is placed at column 6 bank 'b', which is NOT the same strip as the button legs in columns 5 and 7. If the wire actually lands in column 5 or 7 it will connect to the button. The schematic shows pin 2 wired directly to one button terminal, so the intended connection is pin 2 -> one button side.",
   "Black wire runs from Arduino GND to the lower half at about column 5 (row c/d), the same strip as the button's side-1 leg, matching the schematic (button's other terminal to GND).",
   "To match the schematic exactly (pin 2 to one side of the button, GND to the other), the wire placements above should be read as: blue wire on the button's side 2 strip, black wire on side 1 strip. No external resistor is used; the sketch uses INPUT_PULLUP.",
   "The Leonardo's GND pin used is the one highlighted in the image (the first GND next to 5V), mapped to GND.1."
  ]
 },
